2. rpc-websockets 9.3.9 - GNU LGPL v3.0 only
================================================================================

  Upstream   https://github.com/elpheria/rpc-websockets
  Version    9.3.9
  Copyright  Elpheria j.d.o.o.
  License    GNU Lesser General Public License, version 3.0 only

THIS IS THE ONLY COMPONENT IN THE SHIPPED APPLICATION THAT IS NOT UNDER A
PERMISSIVE LICENSE, AND IT IS WHY THIS FILE EXISTS.

How it gets in. Vanta does not depend on it directly. It arrives transitively
through `@solana/web3.js` (MIT), whose browser build does

    import { CommonClient, WebSocket } from 'rpc-websockets';

at module scope. A bundler cannot drop a top-level import it cannot prove is
unused, so the library is compiled into the release APK. This was verified
rather than assumed:

  $ grep -rl max_reconnects_reached dist/assets/*.js
  dist/assets/index.browser.esm-CqbKqh0d.js

  $ unzip -p deploy/releases/vanta-1.0.0-ux10.apk \
      assets/www/assets/index.browser.esm-CqbKqh0d.js | grep -c max_reconnects_reached
  1

What Vanta does not claim. The library is unmodified and is used as a library.
Elpheria holds the copyright. Vanta claims none of it and grants no additional
rights over it.

What the LGPL asks for, and how it is met here:

  (a) Prominent notice that the library is used and that it is covered by the
      LGPL. This section is that notice.

  (b) A copy of the GNU GPL and of the LGPL travels with the distribution.
      See `licenses/GPL-3.0.txt` and `licenses/LGPL-3.0.txt`. LGPL-3.0 is
      written as a set of additional permissions layered on GPL-3.0, which is
      why both texts are shipped.

  (c) The ability to relink. The library is an unmodified npm dependency
      installed by pnpm and compiled into a JavaScript bundle; it is not
      welded into machine code. To replace it with a modified build:

          pnpm install
          # patch or replace node_modules/rpc-websockets, for example with a
          # pnpm patch, then:
          pnpm build:android

      Everything needed is in this repository, which is itself open source
      under Apache-2.0, so there is no application code a user cannot see or
      rebuild. Vanta adds no terms forbidding this and imposes no restriction
      on reverse engineering its own code in order to debug a modification to
      the library (LGPL-3.0 section 4, final paragraph).

  (d) Corresponding source. The library is unmodified. Its source is public at
      the upstream URL above and on npm as `rpc-websockets@9.3.9`. No written
      offer is required for source the recipient already has; `pnpm-lock.yaml`
      pins the exact version and integrity hash.

If you redistribute Vanta or an APK built from it, keep this file and the
`licenses/` directory alongside it.

KNOWN CLEANUP, RECORDED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. The library is bundled only because
`@solana/web3.js` imports it at module scope. Vanta's RPC traffic is plain HTTPS
and never opens a websocket, so aliasing `rpc-websockets` to a stub in the Vite
config would drop it from the bundle and retire this obligation entirely. That
has not been done, because the APK's current build has not been re-tested on
hardware and removing a dependency that is baked into an app we cannot currently
re-verify would trade a license obligation we can meet for a runtime risk we
cannot measure. We took the obligation we can discharge.


================================================================================
3. Solana Mobile webshell template - no upstream license declared
================================================================================

The Android shell in `android/` was generated from the Solana Mobile webshell
CLI template and then substantially modified.

  Generator  @solana-mobile/webshell-cli 0.1.0
  Upstream   https://github.com/solana-mobile/web-shell (directory `template/`)
  License    none declared

Stated plainly, because the honest answer is not the tidy one: as of 2026-09-29
that repository declares `"license": null` in its `package.json` and contains no
LICENSE, COPYING or NOTICE file anywhere in its tree. It is not Apache-2.0, and
this file will not claim that it is. The CLI exists to generate Android projects
for developers to own and ship; that is its documented purpose, and `template/`
is published inside the npm package for exactly that use. Vanta treats the
generated shell as its own work, records the provenance here, and asserts no
rights over any upstream template code. If Solana Mobile publishes a license for
the template, Vanta will follow it. What Vanta changed is listed in
`android/NOTICE`.

Vanta separately depends on Solana Mobile packages that *are* Apache-2.0,
notably `@solana-mobile/wallet-standard-mobile` and
`@solana-mobile/mobile-wallet-adapter-protocol` for Mobile Wallet Adapter, and
follows the Apache-2.0 `solana-mobile/mobile-wallet-adapter` specification.
Those are used unmodified.


================================================================================
4. Everything else
================================================================================

The remaining dependencies are permissive and used unmodified, as installed
from the public npm registry. To reproduce the census:

    pnpm licenses list

Two packages declare no license field but are not a concern. `eyes@0.1.8`
carries an MIT LICENSE file (Copyright (c) 2009 cloudhead), and
`text-encoding-utf-8@1.0.2` is dedicated to the public domain under the
Unlicense. Neither reaches the shipped bundle.

`lightningcss` is declared MPL-2.0. Tailwind runs it at build time to transform
CSS and its code is not distributed inside the APK.

The single LGPL entry in that census is rpc-websockets, covered in section 2.
